<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     EXPERIMENT (t118, 16 Oct 2026) - autoSizeTextType, on ONE widget.

     CHANGED 20 Oct 2026 - THE DATE ROW MOVED UP, from 42dp to 24dp, and the
     retest line from 82dp to 60dp.

     The owner placed a second Last Test widget at the smallest size the
     launcher offers - one launcher row, 75dp tall - and it showed its title
     and NOTHING ELSE. The reason was arithmetic, not a bug: the rows sat at
     42dp and 82dp, and a one-row card has 43dp of content between its 16dp
     paddings, so both rows fell off the bottom and the card rendered as a
     title and an empty box.

     The fix is in the LAYOUT rather than at render time, and that is the
     whole point of putting it here: a row that is moved by a provider has to
     be moved by an action that the host applies, and on this device that
     action (RemoteViews.setFloat on "setTranslationY") moved the Clinic
     Card's location row and did nothing at all to this one - silently, with
     no exception and no other visible difference. A layout margin cannot
     fail that way.

     The 2-cell card keeps all three rows (title, date, retest) and simply
     reads a little tighter, which is the same look every other card in this
     app has now.

     CLAUDE.md and lesson L-110 say autoSizeTextType "does not work in
     RemoteViews". THAT REASONING IS REFUTED, verified in the platform source at
     C:\Users\kanem\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk\sources\android-37.0\android\widget\
     TextView.java:

       - supportsAutoSizeText() (line 13938) returns true UNCONDITIONALLY. It is
         not gated on RemoteViews, on the view class, or on anything else. L-110's
         argument - "RemoteViews cannot inflate a custom TextView subclass" - does
         not bear on the question, because autoSizeTextType needs no subclass; it
         is a framework attribute on the plain TextView, which IS on RemoteViews'
         supported list.
       - setupAutoSizeText() (line 2357) activates on AUTO_SIZE_TEXT_TYPE_UNIFORM
         and nothing else.

     THE REAL BLOCKER IS wrap_content, and it was never the reason L-110 gave.
     Autosize measures `getMeasuredWidth()` (line 11563). Under wrap_content the
     TextView measures to ITS OWN TEXT WIDTH, so availableWidth equals the text
     width and findLargestTextSizeWhichFits always returns the top of the ladder
     - autosize engaged, doing nothing. Under match_parent the measure spec is
     EXACTLY and availableWidth is the real content width. So match_parent is
     REQUIRED and is the entire fix.

     WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED:

     - layout_height stays wrap_content. Autosize also constrains availableHeight
       (line 11566), which looks like a second blocker and is not: with
       wrap_content height and no compound padding, availableHeight equals the
       layout's own height, so the equality test passes at every rung. Changing
       it would break mTotalLength and therefore the ROOT's vertical centring.

     - maxLines stays 1/2. It feeds suggestedSizeFitsInSpace (line 11644), so
       changing it would change the experiment rather than test it.

     - ellipsize and breakStrategy stay. Neither enters the fit test - the
       StaticLayout at line 11635-11649 sets no ellipsize, and applies the same
       getBreakStrategy() as the real layout - so there is no surprise the
       fitter did not model.

     - The provider is NOT touched. TextView.setTextSize is guarded by
       `if (!isAutoSizeEnabled())` (line 4752), so WidgetTextFit's
       setTextViewTextSize becomes a DETERMINISTIC NO-OP once autosize is on -
       not a race, the framework always wins. So leaving the fit() calls in place
       costs nothing, keeps widgetTextFitMaxLinesGuard at its asserted 26 sites,
       and makes its log line a FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION to compare against the
       render.

     THE TWO TRAPS, both verified in source, both of which would look like
     success:

     - autoSizeMaxTextSize defaults to 112sp (line 1115). Omit it and the text
       GROWS to whatever fits, often 30sp+, clipped vertically.
     - autoSizeStepGranularity defaults to 1 PIXEL (line 1117). Omit it and the
       ladder picks sub-sp sizes like 13.4sp, contradicting the whole-sp policy
       WidgetTextFit deliberately chose.

     And the widget-killer: validateAndSetAutoSizeTextTypeUniformConfiguration
     THROWS when autoSizeMaxTextSize <= autoSizeMinTextSize (line 2306). In a
     widget that throw is swallowed by AppWidgetHostView into its error view, so
     the whole widget becomes "Can't load widget". Which is why widget_retest_due
     is left alone below - it declares textSize="12sp", so autosizing it with a
     12sp floor would be exactly that crash.

     THE CONTROL. widget_retest_due is deliberately left on wrap_content with no
     autosize. It is the one field autosize provably cannot help, and leaving it
     gives an old-mechanism row in the SAME process, SAME density, SAME font
     scale, SAME layout pass. Feed the same string to both rows and compare their
     ink heights: identical means autosize did not engage, differing means it
     did. Immune to the cross-process comparison problems that make a screenshot
     alone ambiguous.

     CAVEAT FOR PRODUCTION, not for this experiment: autoSizeTextType is API 27+
     and minSdkVersion is 24 (android/variables.gradle:2), so on API 24-26 the
     attribute is silently ignored and the field falls back to today's behaviour.
-->
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android" android:id="@+id/widget_root" android:layout_width="match_parent" android:layout_height="match_parent" android:padding="16dp" android:background="@drawable/widget_background">

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_test_title"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="top|center_horizontal"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:text="Last test"
        android:textSize="16sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_primary"
        android:textStyle="bold"
        android:maxLines="1"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:breakStrategy="simple"
        android:autoSizeTextType="uniform"
        android:autoSizeMinTextSize="12sp"
        android:autoSizeMaxTextSize="16sp"
        android:autoSizeStepGranularity="1sp" />

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_last_test"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:layout_gravity="top|center_horizontal"
        android:layout_marginTop="24dp"
        android:text="No tests logged"
        android:textSize="14sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_good"
        android:textStyle="bold"
        android:maxLines="2"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:breakStrategy="simple"
        android:autoSizeTextType="uniform"
        android:autoSizeMinTextSize="12sp"
        android:autoSizeMaxTextSize="14sp"
        android:autoSizeStepGranularity="1sp" />

    <!-- THE CONTROL. Deliberately unchanged: wrap_content, no autosize. See the
         header. This is the row that tells us whether autosize engaged at all. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_retest_due"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="top|center_horizontal"
        android:layout_marginTop="60dp"
        android:text="Retest due: —"
        android:textSize="12sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_muted"
        android:maxLines="2"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:breakStrategy="simple" />

</FrameLayout>
